.SS "void schultz_camera_upright (const void * from, uint32_t width, uint32_t height, uint32_t pitch, uint32_t quarters, uint32_t * into)"

.PP
Copies a camera picture into place, turning it upright on the way\&. The turn costs nothing on top of the copy that has to happen anyway: the pixels are being moved from the camera's own rows into a packed block either way, and a turn only changes where each one lands\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIfrom\fP The camera's pixels, ARGB8888\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fIwidth\fP Width of the picture as the camera gave it\&. 
.br
\fIheight\fP Height of the picture as the camera gave it\&. 
.br
\fIpitch\fP Bytes per row in \fCfrom\fP, which a camera pads\&. 
.br
\fIquarters\fP Quarter turns clockwise to apply, 0 to 3\&. 
.br
\fIinto\fP Receives width by height pixels, packed, with the width and height swapped for one and three quarter turns\&. Must not be NULL and must have room for width * height of them\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "uint32_t schultz_camera_quarters (float degrees)"

.PP
How far a frame says it is out, as quarter turns clockwise\&. 
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIdegrees\fP What the frame reported, which may be negative or above a full turn\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
0 to 3\&. Anything that is not a right angle answers 0, because a picture cannot be turned by part of one without resampling it, and a picture the wrong way up is better than a smeared one\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "void schultz_camera_preview_size (float want_width, uint32_t frame_w, uint32_t frame_h, float * out_width, float * out_height)"

.PP
The size a preview should ask to be, given the pictures arriving\&. A widget here cannot tell layout what shape it is: the vtable has no measure, and a leaf takes its size hints and nothing else\&. So a preview says what it wants by setting its own preferred size, and this is the rule it uses\&.
.PP
Declared apart from the camera so it can be checked against numbers rather than against a phone held a particular way round\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIwant_width\fP The width the node actually has, which is not the width it asked for: a column stretches a child across itself and ignores the request\&. Anything not positive falls back to the picture's own width, which is all there is to go on before anything has been laid out\&. 
.br
\fIframe_w\fP Width of the pictures arriving\&. Must not be zero\&. 
.br
\fIframe_h\fP Height of them\&. Must not be zero\&. 
.br
\fIout_width\fP Receives the width to ask for\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fIout_height\fP Receives the height that width implies, from the picture's own proportions\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.RE
.PP

